# L §4.4 (tally 2.1.8 R13, X1; K1, T's fixes/g3/fix2_c2): two calendar rows carry the same added text across their
# governs boundary, so they are two unknowns: A in the row governing 06-30, B in the row governing 07-01. The plain
# line carries A, then B; one is taken at a time. A = 07-01 makes 06-30 a business day: the answer, and B is never
# read. A = 06-30 leaves a window carrying B, which splits in two: three nested lines; the plain line is their
# interval (qp). 2.1.7's refcore read both entries as one unknown: two lines, and window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-01).
=== m.tally
model k1n
  tally 2
  on 2026-03-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source card "invented"

input cal: calendar

ask qp: add_business(2026-06-29, 1, cal)
ask q: add_business(2026-06-29, 1, cal) windows each

version c1
  cite card
  governs ..2026-06-30
  known 2026-01-01
  cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], added [window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-01)]) "c"

version c2
  cite card
  governs 2026-07-01..
  known 2026-01-01
  cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], added [window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-01)]) "c"
=== expect
q [added window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-01) = 2026-06-30, added window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-01) = 2026-06-30]: 2026-07-01
  held: cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun]) (c2), read for 2026-07-01
  cut 2026-03-01
q [added window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-01) = 2026-06-30, added window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-01) = 2026-07-01]: 2026-07-02
  held: cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun]) (c2), read for 2026-07-01
  held: cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun]) (c2), read for 2026-07-02
  cut 2026-03-01
q [added window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-01) = 2026-07-01]: 2026-06-30
  cut 2026-03-01
qp: window(2026-06-30, 2026-07-02)
  held: cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun]) (c2), read for 2026-07-01
  held: cal = calendar(weekends [sat, sun]) (c2), read for 2026-07-02
  cut 2026-03-01
